  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nInverted surface trough along the Appalachians will sharpen today and\nthen pivot westward across the forecast area tonight into Wednesday\nin response to a mid level system that will be closing off as it\ntracks southeast across the region. This will result in showers\ndeveloping and moving west across the area. Cannot rule out a bit of\nthunder. Greater coverage of showers and rainfall amounts will be \nfrom central Ohio into northeast Kentucky.\n\nAs the mid level low continues off to the southeast to then get\nabsorbed into a larger cut off along the Atlantic coast at the end of\nthe week, surface high pressure centered over Quebec will be able to\nspread back south into the region. This will bring an extended period\nof dry conditions.\n\nHigh temperatures will be below normal through the week and into the\nweekend. With increased low level moisture and a stubborn cloud deck,\nthe diurnal range will be narrowed so that low will be a bit above\nnormal tonight and Wednesday night. Low levels will finally dry out\non Thursday and the cloud deck will erode. So lows will get a bit\nchilly for the latter part of the week.",
